// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"AP Euro begins with the Renaissance, a rebirth of classical learning that changed how Europeans saw the world.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Renaissance humanism revived Greek and Roman texts and emphasized human potential, individual achievement and secular (worldly) concerns alongside religion."},{"t":"p","text":"The Italian Renaissance flourished in wealthy city-states like Florence, funded by patrons like the Medici. Artists like Leonardo, Michelangelo and Raphael used perspective and realism. Machiavelli's The Prince argued rulers should be practical and use power effectively rather than idealistically."},{"t":"p","text":"The Northern Renaissance (Erasmus, Thomas More) applied humanism to Christian reform, focusing on scripture and piety."},{"t":"p","text":"The printing press (Gutenberg, around 1450) spread ideas rapidly, increasing literacy and helping the Reformation and Scientific Revolution spread."}]},{"say":"European states expanded overseas for wealth, religion and power, reshaping the world.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Motives: God (spreading Christianity), gold (wealth from spices and precious metals) and glory (national prestige). Technology made it possible: the caravel, astrolabe, compass and improved maps."},{"t":"p","text":"Portugal explored Africa's coast and reached India; Spain colonized the Americas after Columbus (1492). Later, the Dutch, English and French built trading empires."},{"t":"p","text":"Effects: the Columbian Exchange transferred crops, animals and diseases; the Atlantic slave trade forced millions of Africans into slavery; silver from the Americas caused inflation in Europe (the Price Revolution); and economic power shifted from the Mediterranean to the Atlantic."},{"t":"p","text":"Mercantilism guided policy: colonies existed to enrich the mother country, and states sought a favorable balance of trade."}]}],[{"say":"In 1517, a monk's protest split Western Christianity and changed European politics forever.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Martin Luther's 95 Theses (1517) criticized the sale of indulgences. He taught salvation by faith alone and the authority of scripture alone, and rejected papal authority."},{"t":"p","text":"John Calvin taught predestination and created a strict religious community in Geneva; Calvinism spread to France (Huguenots), Scotland (Presbyterians) and the Netherlands."},{"t":"p","text":"In England, Henry VIII broke with Rome to annul his marriage, creating the Church of England (Anglicanism)."},{"t":"p","text":"The printing press spread Protestant ideas quickly. Princes in the Holy Roman Empire adopted Lutheranism partly to gain independence from the emperor and the Church."}]},{"say":"The Catholic Church fought back, and religious divisions led to a century of war.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Catholic (Counter-) Reformation reformed abuses and reaffirmed doctrine at the Council of Trent (1545–1563). The Jesuits (founded by Ignatius of Loyola) spread Catholicism through education and missions."},{"t":"p","text":"Wars of religion:"},{"t":"list","items":["French Wars of Religion between Catholics and Huguenots, ended by the Edict of Nantes (1598), which granted Huguenots limited toleration.","The Thirty Years' War (1618–1648), which devastated the Holy Roman Empire."]},{"t":"p","text":"The Peace of Westphalia (1648) ended the Thirty Years' War, recognized Calvinism, weakened the Holy Roman Emperor, and established the principle of state sovereignty: states control their own religion and affairs."}]}],[{"say":"In the 1600s, many monarchs claimed total power. Louis XIV of France was the model.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Absolutism is a system where the monarch holds supreme power, often justified by divine right. Absolute rulers controlled the nobility, built large armies and bureaucracies, and centralized taxation."},{"t":"list","items":["Louis XIV (\"I am the state\") built Versailles to control the nobility, revoked the Edict of Nantes, and fought costly wars. His minister Colbert promoted mercantilism.","Peter the Great modernized and Westernized Russia, building St. Petersburg.","Prussia built a powerful military state under the Hohenzollerns.","Austria's Habsburgs ruled a diverse empire."]},{"t":"p","text":"The balance of power: European states formed alliances to prevent any one power from dominating."}]},{"say":"England and the Dutch Republic took a different path, limiting their rulers' power.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Constitutionalism limits government through laws and representative institutions."},{"t":"p","text":"England's struggle: conflict between the Stuart kings and Parliament led to the English Civil War (1642–1651), the execution of Charles I, and Oliver Cromwell's rule. After the monarchy was restored, the Glorious Revolution (1688) replaced James II with William and Mary, and the English Bill of Rights (1689) limited royal power and strengthened Parliament."},{"t":"p","text":"John Locke argued government rests on the consent of the governed to protect natural rights."},{"t":"p","text":"The Dutch Republic was a wealthy, commercial state governed by merchants, with religious toleration and a powerful trading empire."}]}],[{"say":"Between about 1550 and 1700, thinkers challenged ancient authorities and built modern science.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Scientific Revolution replaced the geocentric (Earth-centered) model with the heliocentric (Sun-centered) model and used observation, experiment and mathematics."},{"t":"list","items":["Copernicus proposed heliocentrism.","Kepler showed planets move in elliptical orbits.","Galileo used the telescope to support heliocentrism and was tried by the Inquisition.","Newton's laws of motion and universal gravitation explained the universe with math."]},{"t":"p","text":"The scientific method: Francis Bacon emphasized inductive reasoning from observation (empiricism); René Descartes emphasized deductive reasoning and doubt (\"I think, therefore I am\")."}]},{"say":"Enlightenment thinkers applied reason to society, government and religion, inspiring revolutions.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Enlightenment philosophes believed reason could improve society."},{"t":"list","items":["Locke: natural rights and government by consent.","Montesquieu: separation of powers.","Voltaire: religious toleration and free speech.","Rousseau: the social contract and the general will.","Adam Smith: free markets and laissez-faire economics (The Wealth of Nations).","Mary Wollstonecraft: education and rights for women."]},{"t":"p","text":"Ideas spread through salons (often hosted by women), coffeehouses and the Encyclopédie. Deism viewed God as a \"clockmaker\" who created the universe but doesn't intervene."},{"t":"p","text":"Enlightened absolutists like Frederick the Great of Prussia, Catherine the Great of Russia and Joseph II of Austria adopted some reforms while keeping power."}]}],[{"say":"In 1789, France erupted in a revolution that spread ideas of liberty and equality across Europe.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Causes: Enlightenment ideas, a severe financial crisis, an unfair tax system that burdened the Third Estate, food shortages and a weak king (Louis XVI)."},{"t":"p","text":"Phases:"},{"t":"list","items":["Liberal phase (1789–1792): the Estates-General became the National Assembly; the Tennis Court Oath; storming of the Bastille; the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen; a constitutional monarchy.","Radical phase (1792–1794): France became a republic; Louis XVI was executed; Robespierre's Committee of Public Safety led the Reign of Terror.","The Directory (1795–1799): a weak, corrupt government that ended when Napoleon seized power."]},{"t":"p","text":"Olympe de Gouges wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Woman, demanding equality for women."}]},{"say":"Napoleon spread revolutionary ideas by conquest, then Europe's powers tried to restore the old order.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Napoleon Bonaparte seized power in 1799 and crowned himself emperor in 1804. The Napoleonic Code made laws equal for men, protected property and promoted merit, but limited women's rights. The Concordat of 1801 made peace with the Catholic Church."},{"t":"p","text":"He conquered much of Europe but was defeated after a disastrous invasion of Russia (1812) and finally at Waterloo (1815). His conquests spread nationalism and revolutionary ideas."},{"t":"p","text":"The Congress of Vienna (1814–1815), led by Austria's Metternich, restored monarchies, created a balance of power and promoted conservatism. The Concert of Europe tried to suppress future revolutions."}]}],[{"say":"Starting in Britain, machines and factories transformed Europe's economy and society.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Britain industrialized first because of coal and iron, an agricultural revolution that freed labor, capital from trade, colonies, rivers and canals, and political stability."},{"t":"p","text":"Key innovations: the steam engine (James Watt), mechanized textile production (spinning jenny, power loom), and railroads. The Second Industrial Revolution (late 1800s) brought steel, chemicals, electricity and the internal combustion engine."},{"t":"p","text":"Industrialization spread to Belgium, France and Germany, often with more government support."},{"t":"p","text":"Effects: rapid urbanization, a new industrial working class and middle class, and harsh factory conditions including child labor."}]},{"say":"Industrial society inspired new ideas about politics and economics, and movements for reform.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"19th-century ideologies:"},{"t":"list","items":["Liberalism: individual rights, constitutional government and free markets.","Conservatism: tradition, monarchy and the established church.","Nationalism: loyalty to one's nation; led to the unification of Italy (Cavour, Garibaldi) and Germany (Bismarck, 1871).","Socialism: public control of industry to address inequality.","Marxism: Karl Marx's Communist Manifesto (1848) argued class struggle would lead workers to overthrow capitalism."]},{"t":"p","text":"The Revolutions of 1848 swept Europe but mostly failed. Reforms followed: Britain expanded voting rights, passed factory acts limiting child labor, and later women's suffrage movements grew."}]}],[{"say":"The Great War shattered Europe's empires and led to revolution in Russia.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Causes of World War I (MAIN): Militarism, Alliances, Imperialism and Nationalism. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (1914) triggered the alliance system: the Triple Entente (France, Britain, Russia) vs. the Central Powers (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire)."},{"t":"p","text":"Trench warfare on the Western Front caused massive casualties. It was a total war, mobilizing entire economies and using propaganda."},{"t":"p","text":"The Russian Revolution (1917): the tsar was overthrown, and Lenin's Bolsheviks seized power, creating the Soviet Union."},{"t":"p","text":"The Treaty of Versailles (1919) blamed Germany, imposed heavy reparations and took territory, breeding resentment. The Ottoman, Austro-Hungarian and Russian empires collapsed."}]},{"say":"Economic crisis and resentment led to totalitarian dictatorships and the deadliest war in history.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Totalitarian states controlled every aspect of life: Mussolini's fascist Italy, Hitler's Nazi Germany, and Stalin's Soviet Union (with forced collectivization, five-year plans and purges)."},{"t":"p","text":"The Great Depression helped extremists rise. Appeasement (like the Munich Agreement, 1938) failed to stop Hitler. World War II began when Germany invaded Poland in 1939."},{"t":"p","text":"The Holocaust was the Nazi genocide of six million Jews and millions of others (Roma, disabled people, political opponents), carried out in concentration and death camps."},{"t":"p","text":"The Allies defeated Germany in 1945. Afterward, the United Nations was founded, Nazi leaders were tried at Nuremberg, and Europe was divided between East and West."}]}],[{"say":"After World War II, Europe was divided by an \"Iron Curtain,\" and its colonial empires collapsed.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Cold War divided Europe: the West (US-aligned, NATO, capitalist democracies) and the East (Soviet-controlled, Warsaw Pact, communist states)."},{"t":"list","items":["The Marshall Plan rebuilt Western Europe.","The Berlin Wall (1961) symbolized division.","The USSR crushed uprisings in Hungary (1956) and Czechoslovakia (Prague Spring, 1968)."]},{"t":"p","text":"Decolonization: weakened European powers lost their empires. India gained independence from Britain (1947); France fought wars in Vietnam and Algeria; most African colonies became independent in the 1950s and 1960s."},{"t":"p","text":"Western Europe built welfare states with national health care and social insurance."}]},{"say":"Former enemies built a united Europe, and the Cold War ended peacefully.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"European integration began with economic cooperation (the European Coal and Steel Community, 1951, and the European Economic Community) and grew into the European Union (Maastricht Treaty, 1992), with a common market, open borders and, for many members, the euro."},{"t":"p","text":"Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms, glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring), loosened Soviet control. Solidarity in Poland and peaceful protests led to the fall of the Berlin Wall (1989), German reunification (1990) and the collapse of the Soviet Union (1991)."},{"t":"p","text":"Challenges since then include the breakup of Yugoslavia and ethnic cleansing, debates over immigration, Brexit (the UK leaving the EU in 2020), economic crises and Russia's aggression toward its neighbors."}]}]];
export default text;
